<TopicPage slug="subjects/geography/advanced/remote-sensing-and-earth-observation">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Remote sensing is <strong>geography's instrument revolution</strong>: a permanent, global,
    eyes-on-everything observation network orbiting above the weather. It converted geography
    from a field of surveys and estimates into one of continuous planetary measurement — and
    made climate change, deforestation and urbanisation measurable, contestable facts.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the sensor zoo and the discoveries each made possible" widget="equationStep" config={remoteCfg} />

  <h2>Where it's heading</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Daily global imaging:</strong> Planet's constellation photographs every land area daily — crop failures, war damage and wildfires visible within 24 hours. Earth observation became news infrastructure.</li>
    <li><strong>Methane hunting:</strong> instruments like MethaneSAT pinpoint super-emitter leaks from oil, gas and landfills — turning climate accountability from pledges to measurements with coordinates.</li>
    <li><strong>AI meets pixels:</strong> machine learning counts trees, classifies crops, predicts floods and reads refugee-camp populations from imagery. The interpretation layer is now computational (the CS ladder connects directly).</li>
    <li><strong>Privacy and power questions:</strong> persistent global imaging raises surveillance questions — whose fields, ports and bases are being watched, and who decides what the data shows. Remote sensing is geography, ethics and geopolitics at once.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> NASA Landsat Program; ESA Copernicus Sentinel data; IPCC observational chapters; Planet Labs and MethaneSAT mission pages.</p>
</TopicPage>
